From 924c4218cb252433b2909eab5c85784d06a0a1d1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cerXXXX <polyakovia2009@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 23:06:55 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] perf: add --downscale-to-output to render big wallpapers at
 the screen's resolution

Scenes render into framebuffers the size of their orthographic projection
and every image layer with effects gets framebuffers the size of its
texture, so a 3840x2160 scene on a 1920x1080 screen does all its passes at
four times the pixels the screen can show. Videos are likewise rendered to
a texture of the video's size before being scaled to the output.

With --downscale-to-output, a wallpaper gets the pixel size of the output
showing it (the bounding box at the densest screen's pixel ratio for a span
group) and renders content bigger than it at the output's resolution:

- Scenes: the scene framebuffer, the bloom and shadow atlas framebuffers,
  each layer's composite framebuffers and its effects' framebuffers are
  created scaled down; the camera and all geometry stay in scene units, so
  only the pixel count changes. The scale fills the output for fill,
  stretch and default scaling and fits inside it for fit. Framebuffers
  remember their scale, so an image whose texture is a framebuffer (the
  bloom layer reads _rt_FullFrameBuffer) still gets its size in scene units.
- Videos: mpv renders into a texture of the scaled size, which it scales
  the video to itself.
- Screenshots read the wallpaper framebuffer at its real size instead of
  the projection's.

Nothing smaller than the output is scaled up, and without the flag nothing
changes. g_TexelSize stays in scene units, so effects keep their size on
screen.

Measured on Intel Iris Xe, one 1920x1080 output, 30 fps (render engine time
from DRM fdinfo): a 3840x2160 scene with two full-screen effects and bloom
goes from 46.7% to 19.4%, a 3840x2160 H.264 video from 9.5% to 5.3%.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
